<section>
    <h2>The Kyū Unit</h2>

    <p>At the heart of unit.gl is the <strong>Kyū</strong> (級, きゅう) — a Japanese unit of measurement traditionally used
        in typography. One Kyū equals exactly 0.25mm, making it one of the smallest practical units in print design.</p>

    <p>In the digital realm, we redefine the Kyū as:</p>

    <div class="api-signature">1Q = 1/16rem = 0.0625rem ≈ 1px (at 16px base font size)</div>

    <p>This isn't arbitrary. The number 16 is fundamental to digital typography:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>16px</strong> is the default browser font size</li>
        <li><strong>16</strong> divides evenly by 2, 4, and 8 (common grid intervals)</li>
        <li><strong>16-point</strong> grids are industry standard in UI design</li>
        <li><strong>16 Q = 1rem</strong> creates a simple mental model</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The beauty of the Q unit is that it provides pixel-level precision while remaining fully scalable. If a user
        increases their browser font size, your entire layout scales proportionally — without breaking.</p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
        <h4>Why "Kyū"?</h4>
        <p>The word 級 (kyū) means "grade," "level," or "rank" in Japanese. It reflects the hierarchical nature of the
            unit — each Q is a building block that combines to create larger structures. The term also connects unit.gl
            to the rich tradition of Japanese typography and its meticulous attention to proportion.</p>
    </div>
</section>
